# TypeScript Coding Guidelines

Use these conventions when creating or updating TypeScript in this project.

## Naming

- Use `PascalCase` for types, interfaces, enums, and classes.
- Use `camelCase` for functions, methods, properties, and local variables.
- Do not prefix interfaces with `I`.
- Prefer whole words in names over abbreviations.

## Types and Nullability

- Prefer `undefined` over `null`.
- Keep shared types explicit and avoid adding to global scope.
- Export only what must be used across files.

## Style and Constructs

- Prefer arrow functions over anonymous `function` expressions.
- Omit parentheses for single-parameter arrow functions when possible.
- Always use braces for `if`, `else`, and loop bodies.
- Keep opening braces on the same line as the statement.
- Use one variable declaration per statement.

## Comments and Strings

- Use JSDoc comments for exported functions, classes, interfaces, and enums.
- Use double quotes for string literals.

## Practical Notes

- Prefer immutable patterns for arrays and objects unless mutation is required.
- Prefer `forEach`/`map`/`filter` style iteration when it keeps code clear.
